> Inspect the actual board state

The native diagram reads the current object directly. Board games display the five-move worked result; the grid displays its original start, walls and goal.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/board_games_board.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -lpthread -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){namespace e=dm::rl::environments;e::TicTacToe env;env.Reset();for(int a:{0,3,1,4,2})env.Apply(a);int rows=env.rows(),cols=env.cols();dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(32,850);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;style.width=1.3;style.height=1.3;style.font_size=24;for(int r=0;r<rows;++r)for(int c=0;c<cols;++c){int value=env.At(r,c);std::string text=value==0?".":value==1?"X":"O";diagram.node("cell"+std::to_string(r*cols+c),{double(c)*2.2,double(-r)*2.2},text,style);}diagram.save_svg("build/board_games_board.svg");}
```
